.TH tubookmark n 0.2 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tubookmark
.PP
Read and write the \fBNetscape bookmark file format\fR — the HTML interchange format browsers use to import/export bookmarks. A bookmark is a dict \fB{title url folder tags adddate}\fR; \fBfolder\fR is a \fB/\fR\-joined path ("" = top level) and \fBtags\fR is a list. Entity (un)escaping reuses \fBtclutils::tuxml\fR.
.SS API
.CS
set bms [::tclutils::tubookmark::parse $html]          ;# -> list of dicts
set html [::tclutils::tubookmark::serialize $bms -title "My Bookmarks"]
.CE
.PP
Commands:
.IP \(bu
\fBparse html\fR → flat list of bookmark dicts (folder paths reconstructed from the nested \fB<H3>\fR/\fB<DL>\fR structure).
.IP \(bu
\fBserialize bookmarks ?\-title T?\fR → Netscape bookmark HTML, grouping bookmarks into nested folders by their \fBfolder\fR path.
.PP
\fBparse\fR reads the document tag by tag, so the standard export layout (one \fB<DT>\fR per line) and compact files (several links, or \fB<H3>\fR/\fB<DL>\fR and links, on one line) give the same result; every \fB<A>…</A>\fR is one bookmark. Entities in the title and in the \fBHREF\fR, \fBTAGS\fR and \fBADD_DATE\fR values are unescaped; \fBTAGS\fR is split at commas.
.PP
\fBserialize\fR escapes \fB&\fR, \fB<\fR, \fB>\fR in titles and folder names, and additionally \fB"\fR (as \fB&quot;\fR) in the \fBHREF\fR, \fBTAGS\fR and \fBADD_DATE\fR attribute values. Every header line starts at column 0.
